    "text": "RESULTS · FIGURE 14 · VUAA1-REFERENCED RESPONSES\n\nEach construct uses its own DMSO and VUAA1 controls to scale the Test 2 response map, with DMSO at 0 and VUAA1 at 1.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nHARDWARE · FIGURE 18 · LIVING-CELL INTEGRATION\n\nFour wells compare cells-plus-medium and VUAA1 time courses, normalized to each trace's first reading, with a late-window contrast.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nDESCRIPTION · SENSE\n\nDrosophila OR/Orco in HEK293 with GCaMP fluorescence, using VUAA1 as a functional positive control.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nENGINEERING · WET LAB DIAGNOSTIC LOGIC\n\nIonomycin × VUAA1 matrix separates reporter vs channel failure modes.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nSAFETY & SECURITY · CHEMICAL SAFETY\n\nChemical handling covers concentrated VOC stocks, VUAA1 and DMSO, with source-specific safety review, ventilation, protective equipment and waste management.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nEXPERIMENTS · REAGENT PREPARATION\n\nThe reference preparation specifies a 50 mM VUAA1 stock in DMSO at −20 °C and fresh VOC working dilutions. P2 stimulus metadata are documented separately.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nEXPERIMENTS · REFERENCE RESPONSE ANALYSIS\n\nThe cited relative-response method defines R=(Fmax−F0)/F0 and same-plate VUAA1 normalization. Acquired P2 kinetics and endpoint microscopy use their own linked analysis methods.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nRESULTS · ODOR TESTING · TEST 1 AND TEST 2\n\nCompare time courses and control-referenced response maps across ten OR + ORCO conditions, ORCO-only cells and untransfected cells.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nDESCRIPTION · WHY BIOLOGICAL OLFACTION?\n\nOdorant to OR/Orco to calcium to GCaMP fluorescence to optical output. Heterologous Drosophila sensing is published prior work; AeroSense asks how to integrate it.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nDESCRIPTION · HOW THE LAYERS DEPEND\n\nAeroSense is one chain: construct, functional sensing, VOC characterization, measurement, decode, integration, then food context.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nCONTRIBUTION · BIOLOGICAL SENSING PACKAGE\n\nAdaptation map: published OR/Orco precedent and the AeroSense implementation in HEK293. Construct architecture, reproducible workflows and available biological evidence are linked separately.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nENGINEERING · WET LAB CYCLE 2\n\nPreliminary weak fluorescence in the Orco–IRES–GCaMP6 design prompted hypotheses about reporter abundance and localization, with controls needed to separate the causes.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nEXPERIMENTS · EXPRESSION / FUNCTIONAL VALIDATION\n\nFixed HEK293 endpoint images and P2 fluorescence time courses are archived in Results. Expression readouts and functional-response controls are assessed separately.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nEXPERIMENTS · 384-WELL ASSAY SPECIFICATION\n\nA literature-based 384-well specification uses 20 µL buffer, 7 µL vehicle and 9 µL of 4× VOC. It is distinct from the acquired manual-addition 96-well P2 pilot.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nEXPERIMENTS · CONTROLS\n\nReference controls distinguish delivery stress, expression and receptor/reporting function. DNA quality, recovery medium and the separate P2 control analysis are documented.\n\nOPEN SECTION\n\nRESULTS · FLUORESCENCE DATA AND DOWNLOADS\n\nDownload the P2 archive of 32 exports and 3,840 readings, plus endpoint-image inputs, analysis scripts, source hashes and object-level tables.\n\nOPEN SECTION",
